A||N||8||vinnywolfe||v6 dogbone bushings?||||5||
Z||000000||vinnywolfe||07-15-2000||06:50 PM||||i was wondering if anyone has replaced the bushings in the dogbone with the poly.?  is there a noticeable difference or is it mostly eye candy?<BR>also, where, other than the fiero store, can you find them? is there a difference in them from 86-87, or are all v6 dogbones the same?<P>thanks for any feedback,<BR>vinny<BR><P>------------------<BR>||||reg||5
Z||000001||Taijiguy||07-15-2000||07:00 PM||||I bought 'em, and there's a definite difference. There's alot more vibration transmitted to the car, but there's virtually no flex in the motor, so the response feels much more crisp.<BR>The kit consists of four bushing halvs, and two inserts. I have to wonder if regular leaf spring bushings wouldn't work, they're the same type setup. You may be able to match them up, but then, the kit is only what? twenty bucks or so? How much is your time worth to try and make the match. <P>-Taiji||||reg||1
Z||000002||fiero56||07-15-2000||08:23 PM||||I have the ultimate dogbone in my Formula. It greatly reduced the engine flex back there(still too much for my taste though). I don't notice any difference in vibration over the stock rubber bushings(still don't feel the engine running). It does take about a week for the bushings to "soften", meaning the car will vibrate from the engine for the first few days.<P>You can also get bushings from: <A HREF="http://www.suspension.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.suspension.com</A> <P><BR>Figure I should add, you can only replace the v-6 dogbone bushings on dogbones with the "rib" running on the outside of the dogbone. I'm pretty sure only '88 dogbones are different(rib runs  the inside of the dogbone, and the bushings are oval, rather than a perfect circle).<p>[This message has been edited by fiero56 (edited 07-15-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000003||vinnywolfe||07-15-2000||09:07 PM||||hey guys, the dogbone that i am looking for is off of an 87 GT.  it has a SMOOTH outside and a rib INSIDE?!  it looks like the bushing would have to be in the shape of an oval/circle?  the poly bushings look like they are made to fit in something cylindrical?<P>HELP!<P>vinny<P>------------------<BR>||||reg||4
Z||000004||penetrator||07-17-2000||09:23 AM||||I bought the bushings from Fierostore.<BR>The only differnce i noticed was more noise in the car, and i can't say what all noises are, and in 1st and 2nd gear at 1500rpm there is so high vibration transmitted in the coupé so its sounds like the whole trasnmission is broken. And their are red !!??||||reg||1
Z||000005||vinnywolfe||07-17-2000||05:46 PM||||i'm wondering how many other people that have these poly bushings experience more "noise" (i.e. rattles, squeakes)?  if there is no improvement in performance (does it transfer more torque to the wheels?).  then i just might as well pay the dealer $41.20 for a stock one (i can't find anyone else who carries them).  <BR>on the other hand, the ultimate dog bone (fiero store) is only about $10 more and it sure do look perty.<P>confused (as usual),<P>vinny<BR>  <P>------------------<BR>||||reg||5
Z||000006||fiero56||07-17-2000||06:08 PM||||The dobgone prevents the upper half of the engine from rocking forward and back. With a poly dogbone, engine movement is greatly reduced. Allows for much smoother and quicker jumps off the line(I can easily break them loose again <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">), and allows for "stronger" shifts(reduced engine movement). I have an '88 v-6(internally balanced) and engine vibrations are not transmitted any more strongly with the poly dogbone than stock rubber. I still don't feel the engine running at stop lights, and there is no engine vibration anywhere in the rpm range.||||reg||1
Z||000007||vinnywolfe||07-17-2000||09:43 PM||||OK! you've convinced me. i'll buy the ultimate dogbone.  what the hell, whats fifty bucks?!?!<P>thanks for the info,<P>vinny<P>------------------<BR>||||reg||10
Z||000008||fiero56||07-17-2000||09:58 PM||||I really like my ultimagte dogbone. While I was at it, I replaced both tranny mounts. <P>The Iron Duke is a much rougher running engine. It vibrates quite a bit with rubber bushings, and the poly bushings really let you know the engine is running. Plus, anything not bolted down tight inside the car will squeak. Just giving fair warning to Duke owners. 87ECO5 can attest.||||reg||1
